W: 1 At even, ere the sun was set,
W: the sick, O Lord, around thee lay;
W: O in what divers pains they met!
W: O with what joy they went away!
W:
W: 2 Once more &apos;tis eventide, and we
W: oppressed with various ills draw near;
W: what if thy form we cannot see?
W: we know and feel that thou art here.
W:
W: 3 O Saviour Christ, our woes dispel;
W: for some are sick, and some are sad,
W: and some have never loved thee well,
W: and some have lost the love they had;
W:
W: 4 And some have found the world is vain,
W: yet from the world they break not free;
W: and some have friends who give them pain,
W: yet have not sought a friend in thee;
W:
W: 5 And none, O Lord, have perfect rest,
W: for none are wholly free from sin;
W: and they who fain would serve thee best
W: are conscious most of wrong within.
W:
W: 6 O Saviour Christ, thou too art man;
W: thou hast been troubled, tempted, tried;
W: thy kind but searching glance can scan
W: the very wounds that shame would hide.
W:
W: 7 Thy touch has still its ancient power;
W: no word from thee can fruitless fall:
W: Hear, in this solemn evening hour,
W: and in thy mercy heal us all.
W:</miscellaneous-field>
